# Hourly disk maintenance for the forge host, two passes with different
# triggers — see disk-floor-sweep.sh for the exact order and the hard
# bounds:
#   * EVERY run prunes the SYSTEM daemon's per-train `boss-ci:<sha>`
#     images older than six hours (keeping the newest three). Nothing
#     else ever removes one, and until 2026-09-10 only a below-floor run
#     did: 81GB of them on 2026-09-05 (backlog e5dc60e4).
#   * BELOW BOSS_DISK_FLOOR_GB it goes on to the emergency remediations,
#     regenerable caches only, stopping as soon as the floor is met.
# An unmet floor after every bounded remediation is a FAILED unit,
# deliberately — and so is an age pass that could not reach the daemon
# it was asked to prune, because the alternative is a sweep that reports
# success and frees nothing.
#
# Install (forge host): in install.sh's UNITS list, so —
#   ssh <forge> 'cd /home/david/boss && git pull && sudo infra/forge/install.sh'
# or by hand, once:
#   sudo cp /home/david/boss/infra/forge/disk-floor-sweep.{service,timer} /etc/systemd/system/
#   sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl enable --now disk-floor-sweep.timer
[Unit]
Description=BOSS forge: prune aged per-train CI images, and keep the root volume above the disk floor (regenerable caches only)

[Service]
EnvironmentFile=/etc/boss/sor.env
Type=oneshot
# THE SWEEP FLOOR SITS ABOVE THE CI FLOOR. The script's default floor
# (70) is the same number the locomotive refuses under
# (BOSS_CI_MIN_FREE_GB, 70), so a sweep reclaimed to 70 and stopped —
# and the next train's build ate the margin before the check ran:
# 2026-09-05, 74GB free at 02:07 after the sweep, 65GB at 02:55 when
# train #204 was refused at the door, with the sweep declaring itself
# done at exactly the floor an hour earlier. A floor only buys headroom
# if it is HIGHER than the one being defended. 100 leaves ~30GB — three
# to five train+converge cycles (5-9GB each, measured) — between sweeps.
# The script stops at the floor and touches only regenerable caches, so
# a higher number costs nothing when the disk is already above it.
Environment=BOSS_DISK_FLOOR_GB=100
# WHICH SYSTEM OF RECORD THE PRUNE ASKS. The routine pass collects a
# per-train CI image whose `pr-train` packet is CLOSED, which it can only
# know by reading the record; unset, it falls back to the age window that
# could legally collect nothing at the current train rate (backlog
# 9195a2a6). BOSS_JOBS_URL comes from /etc/boss/sor.env (EnvironmentFile=
# under [Service]), rendered by install.sh from infra/estate/estate.toml
# — one spelling for this unit, its wrap pair and every other unit on
# the host (backlog 5222163e; until 2026-09-18 it was pinned inline on
# each Exec line and once more as an Environment=). There is no default
# in the script: a read against a wrong instance answers "no trains"
# instead of erroring. An unreachable or unset URL costs reclaim, never
# safety: the pass prunes less, and the below-floor remediations owe
# the record nothing.
# Rootless docker, same as cluster-deploy-runner; the script defaults
# DOCKER_HOST itself.
User=david
# WHAT THE RUN DID, ON ITS OWN PACKET (backlog 37742794). The sweep
# leaves the floor, free space before and after, what the per-train
# image pass freed and how it ended in this file; boss-step.sh
# (ExecStopPost) merges it onto the `run` step and deletes it. Declared
# ONCE, here, so producer and consumer cannot disagree about the path
# (the step helper's header has the reasoning). In david's home, the
# converge runner's
# choice for the same account: not a shared temp directory, which
# another account on this host could plant a file in.
Environment=BOSS_RUN_SUMMARY_FILE=/home/david/.boss-disk-floor-sweep.summary.json
# A cold builder-cache prune can take minutes; nowhere near the
# converge's build times, but not seconds either.
TimeoutStartSec=30min
# The wrap pair: the timer is the EXECUTOR, the Job is the VISIBILITY
# (timers-leave-a-packet). A failed sweep — floor unmet after every
# bounded remediation — completes nothing, so the packet stays open on
# the fleet view until a later run succeeds or a human decides.
# The helpers refuse without BOSS_JOBS_URL; the EnvironmentFile above
# gives every Exec line the same one.
# THE TOKEN'S HEADER FILE NEVER OUTLIVES A RUN (backlog df38075a, F1;
# the second car, which took the class and not the six units the item
# named). The wrap pair on the Exec lines below presents the machine
# token, and infra/lib/secret-header.sh makes its 0600 header file in
# $RUNTIME_DIRECTORY when a unit declares one: /run/disk-floor-sweep,
# tmpfs, 0700, this unit's account's, removed by systemd when this
# oneshot stops whatever ended it. Without it the file was made in the
# host's shared /tmp, where a killed run left it. The reasoning is in
# infra/forge/estate-observe-units.service; the pin that walks every
# unit is a_killed_sender_leaves_no_header_behind.rs.
RuntimeDirectory=disk-floor-sweep
RuntimeDirectoryMode=0700
ExecStartPre=-/home/david/boss/infra/boss-maintenance-wrap.sh maintenance-disk-floor-sweep "Forge disk floor sweep"
ExecStart=/home/david/boss/infra/forge/disk-floor-sweep.sh
ExecStopPost=-/home/david/boss/infra/boss-step.sh maintenance-disk-floor-sweep run
